The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (eeh_has_flag(EEH_PROBE_MODE_DEV))
    eeh_ops->probe(pdn, NULL);
```
Reason:
The probe operation is called before checking for outstanding krefs, which means that if a device has an existing PE, the probe will exit without performing the necessary cleanup and reinitialization. This results in devices with outstanding krefs not being properly rediscovered by EEH after a complete hotplug.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
/*
 * The EEH cache might not be removed correctly because of
 * unbalanced kref to the device during unplug time, which
 * relies on pcibios_release_device(). So we have to remove
 * that here explicitly.
 */
if (edev->pdev) {
    eeh_rmv_from_parent_pe(edev);
    eeh_addr_cache_rmv_dev(edev->pdev);
    eeh_sysfs_remove_device(edev->pdev);
    edev->mode &= ~EEH_DEV_SYSFS;

    edev->mode |= EEH_DEV_NO_HANDLER;

    edev->pdev = NULL;
    dev->dev.archdata.edev = NULL;
}

if (eeh_has_flag(EEH_PROBE_MODE_DEV))
    eeh_ops->probe(pdn, NULL);
```
Reason:
The patch moves the probe operation to occur after the check and handling of outstanding krefs. This ensures that if a PE exists, it is properly destroyed before the probe is called, allowing affected devices to be correctly rediscovered by EEH after a complete hotplug.

